        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Simón Bolívar</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1783 CE – 1830 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">24 July 1783 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, capital of the Captaincy General of Venezuela; fourth and youngest child of Juan Vicente Bolívar y Ponte and María de la Concepción Palacios y Blanco, one of the wealthiest criollo families in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">17 December 1830, aged 47, of tuberculosis, at the Quinta de San Pedro Alejandrino near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Dictator of the Second Republic of Venezuela (1814); President of the Republic of Colombia, known as Gran Colombia (elected 1819; resignation accepted 1830); dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> (from 1824); named president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> (1825)</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a>; the crossing of the Andes and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> in 1819; founding the Republic of Colombia (Gran Colombia); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> Letter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-manifesto">Cartagena Manifesto</a> and Angostura Address; the title El Libertador; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> being named after him</span></div>
