    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into one of the wealthiest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> families of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and orphaned as a child, Bolívar swore in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1805 to end Spanish rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</li><li>After two failed Venezuelan republics and exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, he crossed the Andes in 1819 and, with his allies, defeated the Spanish in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, Venezuela, Ecuador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> by 1825.</li><li>He united Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, Ecuador and Panama as the Republic of Colombia, but grew increasingly authoritarian and disillusioned, and died in 1830 as the union broke apart.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>On 25 September 1828 Bolívar escaped assassins in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> when Manuela Sáenz bought him time to flee the Palacio de San Carlos. He spent the night hiding under a bridge until loyal soldiers rescued him.</p></aside>
    </section>

