    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">On the Mons Sacer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, Bolívar swears to end Spanish rule in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1813</span><span class="rf-text">He launches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a>, issues the Decree of War to the Death, enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and is named &quot;The Liberator&quot;</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1815</span><span class="rf-text">He resigns his command in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, sails into exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and writes the Jamaica Letter</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1819</span><span class="rf-text">He crosses the Andes, routs the Royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> and is elected president of the new Republic of Colombia</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1821</span><span class="rf-text">With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a> he decisively defeats the Royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo">Battle of Carabobo</a> and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1824</span><span class="rf-text">Named dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> by its congress, he defeats José de Canterac at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-junin">Battle of Junín</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1828</span><span class="rf-text">He assumes supreme power as &quot;president-liberator&quot; of Colombia and survives an assassination attempt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1830</span><span class="rf-text">His resignation is accepted in April; he dies of tuberculosis near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> in December</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-bello.jpg" alt="Andrés Bello" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello">Andrés Bello</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AndresBello.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raymond Monvoisin, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Simón Bolívar was born on 24 July 1783 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, capital of the Captaincy General of Venezuela, the fourth and youngest child of Juan Vicente Bolívar y Ponte and María de la Concepción Palacios y Blanco. The first of the family in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, a notary from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>'s Basque Country also named Simón de Bolívar, had arrived in Venezuela in the 1580s, and by 1783 the Bolívars were among the wealthiest and most prestigious criollo (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a>) families in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish Americas</a>. The British historian John Lynch described his childhood as "at once privileged and deprived". His father died of tuberculosis on 19 January 1786 and his mother on 6 July 1792. Following colonial custom the children were raised by African house slaves; Simón was raised by a slave named Hipólita.</p><p>Notoriously unruly as a child, he was taught by the educator Simón Rodríguez and the intellectual <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bello">Andrés Bello</a>, among others. In 1799 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, where he was educated in the Classics, literature and social studies. There he met María Teresa Rodríguez del Toro y Alaysa, daughter of another wealthy Caracas creole; they married on 26 May 1802 and sailed for Venezuela, where she died of yellow fever on 22 January 1803. Bolívar later said that he swore never to remarry.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that year. He was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was proclaimed Emperor of the French in 1804, and in April 1805 he set out with Rodríguez on a Grand Tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. On 18 August 1805, on the Mons Sacer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, he swore to end Spanish rule in the Americas. He was back in Venezuela in June 1807.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda.jpg" alt="Francisco de Miranda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_de_Miranda_by_Tovar_y_Tovar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Tovar y Tovar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s invasion of the Iberian peninsula in 1807–08 weakened Spanish authority. On 19 April 1810 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> deposed the government of Vicente Emparán and created a Supreme Junta. Bolívar joined its diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which won no recognition, but he persuaded the revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> to return. Congress declared Venezuela's independence on 5 July 1811. After an earthquake devastated the first republic on 26 March 1812, a Royalist army overran the west. Bolívar lost the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>, Miranda capitulated, and Bolívar left for Curaçao and then Cartagena.</p><p>In the service of the United Provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> he issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-manifesto">Cartagena Manifesto</a> and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdalena-river">Magdalena River</a>. On 14 May 1813 he launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a>, entered Caracas on 6 August and was named "The Liberator". He became dictator of a Second Republic on 2 January 1814, but it was overwhelmed by slave revolts and the llanero army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-tomas-boves">José Tomás Boves</a>, who occupied Caracas on 16 July. After further service in New Granada, Bolívar sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in May 1815, where he wrote the Jamaica Letter.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Alexandre Pétion</a> gave him money and military supplies after Bolívar pledged to free every slave in the areas he occupied. A first expedition in 1816 failed. Returning to Venezuela at the end of that year, he joined the Guayana campaign; Angostura fell on 17 July 1817 and became the provisional Republican capital, and on 30 January 1818 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a>, who controlled most of the western Llanos, recognised him as supreme leader.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca.jpg" alt="Battle of Boyacá" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Boyaca_de_Martin_Tovar_y_Tovar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martin Tovar y Tovar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The congress that met at Angostura on 15 February 1819 heard Bolívar advocate a centralised government modelled on the British and elected him president the next day. On 27 May he marched toward the Andes. The army made a gruelling crossing of the Eastern Range, descended into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a> on 6 July and routed the Royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-boyaca">Battle of Boyacá</a> on 7 August; Bolívar entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> three days later. On 17 December 1819 the congress decreed the Republic of Colombia, uniting Venezuela, New Granada and the still Spanish-held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a>, with Bolívar as president.</p><p>After a truce with the Spanish general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo">Pablo Morillo</a> in November 1820, fighting resumed, and on 24 June 1821 Bolívar and Páez decisively defeated the Royalists at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo">Battle of Carabobo</a>. Panama declared independence that November and joined Colombia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pichincha">Battle of Pichincha</a> on 24 May 1822 and occupied Quito, and Bolívar secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayaquil">Guayaquil</a> for Colombia before meeting the Argentine general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> there in July; their conference produced no agreement.</p><p>Asked several times by the Peruvian congress to take command, Bolívar reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> on 1 September 1823 and was named dictator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> on 10 February 1824. He defeated José de Canterac at Junín on 6 August, and on 9 December Sucre defeated the viceroy's army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> and accepted the surrender of all Royalist forces in Peru. On 6 August 1825 a congress in Chuquisaca declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> the nation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, named Bolívar its president and asked him to write a constitution, which was ratified with modification in July 1826.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-piar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-piar.jpg" alt="Manuel Piar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/manuel-piar">Manuel Piar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Carlos_Piar_2012_000.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pablo W. Hernández (1890-1928), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bolívar's methods and ambitions made enemies among Republicans as well as Royalists. In 1812 he was among the officers who arrested Miranda at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-guaira">La Guaira</a> on charges of treason after Miranda's capitulation; Miranda passed into Spanish custody and died in a prison in Cádiz in 1816, and Bolívar received a passport for his role in the arrest. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a> he issued the Decree of War to the Death, ordering the death of all Spaniards in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> not actively aiding his forces.</p><p>Rival commanders resisted him repeatedly. In 1814 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-piar">Manuel Piar</a> declared him a traitor and José Félix Ribas accused him of treachery and exiled him, and in 1816 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-marino">Santiago Mariño</a> and José Francisco Bermúdez deposed him. In 1817 Piar began fomenting rebellion, alleging that Bolívar had dismissed him because of his mulatto heritage; he was captured and executed by firing squad at Angostura on 16 October. In 1824 the Colombian congress stripped Bolívar of his authority for accepting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Peru's elites chafed at his rule and the presence of his soldiers, and in 1827 his opponents there deposed him and repealed his constitution.</p><p>His political ideas drew the same charge. He held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a> needed a strong, centralised government, thought it necessary to rule as a dictator to avoid racial conflict and civil war, and intended to be president for life. The first historical evaluations of him were negative, criticising his conduct of the war, the execution of Piar, the betrayal of Miranda and his authoritarianism, and these criticisms endure in studies of him.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander.jpg" alt="Francisco de Paula Santander" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Santander_by_Acevedo_Bernal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ricardo Acevedo Bernal, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bolívar left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in September 1826 in response to pleas for his return to Colombia, where Páez had revolted against Vice President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-paula-santander">Francisco de Paula Santander</a>. He granted Páez an amnesty and confirmed his authority in Venezuela in January 1827; the amnesty, and clashes over Colombia's finances, turned his differences with Santander into open enmity. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-ocana">Convention of Ocaña</a> in 1828, which seemed likely to adopt a federalist system, his allies walked out and left it without a quorum. On 27 August 1828 he assumed supreme power as "president-liberator" and abolished the vice presidency.</p><p>On 25 September 1828 a group of young liberals tried to assassinate him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a>; Manuela Sáenz, bought time for his escape. Santander, whom Bolívar thought responsible, was pardoned but exiled, and Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-prudencio-padilla">José Prudencio Padilla</a>, though uninvolved, was executed for an earlier rebellion. While Bolívar was away in 1829 his ministers planned for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> prince to succeed him as King of Colombia, a widely unpopular plan that he ordered stopped. Venezuelans voted to secede from Colombia.</p><p>He submitted his resignation to the congress that met in Bogotá on 20 January 1830; it was accepted on 27 April. He left Bogotá on 8 May, meaning to go into exile, and reached Cartagena in late June. With his health failing and no ship forthcoming, he was moved to the Quinta de San Pedro Alejandrino near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a>, where he died of tuberculosis on 17 December 1830, aged 47. That year he declared to the Venezuelan general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-jose-flores">Juan José Flores</a>, "all who have served the Revolution have plowed the sea."</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bolívar is regarded as a hero and a national and cultural icon throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a>, and in the English-speaking world he is known as Latin America's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> and the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela bear his name, as do their currencies, the boliviano and the bolívar. His body was interred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-marta">Santa Marta</a> in 1830; in 1842 Páez secured its return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, and in October 1876 it was moved to the National Pantheon of Venezuela.</p><p>From 1842 popular opinion of him in Venezuela became overwhelmingly positive and grew into what scholars have described as the "cult of Bolívar", led by successive heads of state. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugo-chavez">Hugo Chávez</a> made extensive use of his image and changed the country's official name to the Bolivarian Republic of Venezuela. Chávez also claimed that Bolívar had been poisoned; a commission exhumed the remains on 16 July 2010, and its results, made public in 2011, were inconclusive. In Colombia, allegiance or opposition to Bolívar formed the bedrock of the Conservative and Liberal parties respectively, and in 1974 the insurgent 19th of April Movement stole a sword said to be his from his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> residence.</p><p>His writings remain central to his reputation: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> Letter has been called a "blueprint of Bolívar's political thought" and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cartagena-manifesto">Cartagena Manifesto</a> "one of the great documents of Latin American history". He hoped for a federation of Latin American republics, and at Angostura argued for the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. In 1978 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> created the International Simón Bolívar Prize.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Simón Bolívar, a wealthy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creole</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>, led the wars that freed Venezuela, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, Ecuador, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> between 1810 and 1825 and joined several of them in the Republic of Colombia. His rule turned authoritarian, his union broke apart, and he died in 1830 on his way into exile.</em></p>

